--TEST--
`Core\Test::answerSocket` plays a scripted peer's frames in order and records every frame the program sent, with nothing connecting — `rule:testing/an-outbound-socket-is-answered-by-a-scripted-peer`, `rule:http-server/an-outbound-socket-is-opened-like-an-outbound-call`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The keystone every later socket case is written against: a socket opened to
// a scripted URL completes its handshake without connecting, receives what the
// table holds in the order it holds it, and what the program wrote is read back
// off the table rather than off the call. Nothing below is granted
// `net.connect` and no socket is opened, exactly as
// `rule:testing/an-outbound-call-is-answered-from-a-table` arranges for a call.
Core\Test::answerSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/v1", ["hello", "second"], {protocol: "chat"});

var $socket = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/v1", {protocols: ["chat", "json"]});

// The subprotocol is the peer's choice out of what was offered, reported by the
// socket rather than guessed at from the offer.
echo "protocol=", $socket->protocol() ?? "-", "\n";

// Both directions over one socket, and the sends come first so the recorded
// order below is the program's own rather than a reply to anything.
$socket->send("subscribe");
$socket->sendBytes("ok" as bytes);

// The peer's frames, and `null` once it has closed — which is the reading that
// ends the loop rather than a count the program had to know in advance.
var $message = $socket->receive();
while ($message != null) {
    echo "peer ", $message->text() ?? "-", "\n";
    $message = $socket->receive();
}
$socket->close();

// What the program said, in the one order it said it, as the same
// `Core\Socket\Message` the peer's own frames arrive in: `text` for a `send`
// and `bytes` for a `sendBytes`, which is what says which member wrote it.
foreach (Core\Test::sentSocket() as Core\Socket\Message $frame) {
    if ($frame->text() != null) {
        echo "sent text ", $frame->text() ?? "-", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "sent bytes ", Core\Bytes::length($frame->bytes() ?? ("" as tainted bytes)), "\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
protocol=chat
peer hello
peer second
sent text subscribe
sent bytes 2
